  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nPatchy fog is possible this morning, mainly across central and\neast-central Ohio. Fog is primarily occurring in river valleys.\nTemperatures are generally in the low to mid 30s, but some of\nthe colder spots are getting down into the mid 20s (Wooster is\nat 26, and Ravenna/Portage is down to 27), which may yield\nfreezing fog. Latest Nighttime Microphysics satellite imagery\ndoes show that fog may be starting to form, but no observations\nare reporting reduced visibility yet. Will continue to monitor \nand issue a special weather statement or dense fog advisory \nthis morning if fog.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA 1036 mb high is centered over the mid-Atlantic region and will\nremain there as it dissipates to around 1028 mb. Steady\nsouthwest flow on the backside of this high will advect warmer\nweather to the region as we see highs in the low 50s today climb\nthrough the week and peak in the mid 60s by Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLow pressure moves into the northern Great Lakes by Sunday with\na cold front moving east across the area with rain showers\npossibly accompanying the front. The low pressure system will be\nin a weakening state as its parent upper-level trough lifts\nnortheast and flattens. The most likely scenario is scattered\nlight, rain showers, which max rainfall amounts of 0.25\".",
